---
title: Final interceptors
description: The stage that always runs — on success, on failure, and the one case where it does not.
---

A final interceptor runs last, whether the dispatch succeeded or failed. It returns nothing,
because by the time it runs the dispatch's answer is already settled.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandFinalInterceptor                        // every command
ICommandFinalInterceptor<TCommand>              // one command
ICommandFinalInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>     // typed result
```

Queries have the same three; events have the ones without a result.

## The shape

```csharp
public sealed class RecordOutcome(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, succeeded: exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Both the result and the exception are nullable, and both can be `null` at once — a void
pipeline that succeeded has neither a meaningful result nor a failure. Read them as a pair:

| `exception` | `result` | What happened |
|---|---|---|
| `null` | a value | the handler succeeded |
| `null` | `null` | a void pipeline succeeded |
| set | `null` | it failed and nothing answered |
| set | a value | it failed and the exception stage answered |

## The one case where it does not run

`context.Abort()`.

An abort is a deliberate stop rather than a failure, so it skips the exception stage **and this
one**, and travels to the caller unchanged.

{% aside type="caution" %}
This matters when a pre-interceptor opened something that the final stage was going to close.
If any interceptor can abort after that point, the resource leaks.

Two ways out: put the aborting check ahead of whatever opens the resource, with a higher
`[Weight]` — or open it in the handler where a `try`/`finally` covers every exit.
{% /aside %}

## Pairing with a pre-interceptor

The common shape is a pre-interceptor that records something and a final one that reads it:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("startedAt", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class StopTimer(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("startedAt", out var started))
            metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Use `TryGet` rather than `Get`. The final interceptor may run for a dispatch where the pre one
never did — a different group, or an interceptor added later — and it should not throw while
cleaning up.

## Ordering

`[Weight]` descending, direct before covariant. When a final interceptor closes something,
give it a weight that puts it after anything that reads what it closes.

## Do not throw from here

An exception from a final interceptor escapes into whatever the pipeline was already doing. If
the dispatch was failing, you have replaced a meaningful failure with a bookkeeping one.

Wrap anything that can fail:

```csharp
try
{
    await _audit.WriteAsync(row, context.CancellationToken);
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    _logger.LogWarning(ex, "audit write failed");
}
```

Also be careful with the cancellation token here: if the dispatch failed *because* it was
cancelled, the token is already cancelled and any work you start with it fails immediately.

## Typical uses

**Timing and metrics** for every outcome.

**Audit rows** recording that an operation was attempted, with its result.

**Releasing something** a pre-interceptor took — subject to the abort caveat above.

**Committing or rolling back a transaction**, which is exactly the both-paths shape this stage
exists for. See the [Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## What does not belong here

**Changing the answer.** It returns `void`; the answer is settled. Use post or exception.

**Work the caller waits on that is not part of the operation.** A final interceptor is awaited
inside the dispatch, so slow work here slows every call. Queue it instead.
